  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Humanitarian Assistance for Myanmar Muslims from Rakhine State and Special At-Risk Groups in Thailand</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Since the military takeover in February 2021, Myanmar has faced a sharp increase in internal conflict and displacement, with approximately one-third of the population in need of humanitarian assistance. The worsening conditions in Myanmar have had a significant impact in neighbouring Thailand. Flow monitoring data by the International Organization for Migration (IOM) suggests an increase in entries into Thailand from Myanmar, mostly through irregular channels, and deportations though the total number of Myanmar Muslims from Rakhine State in detention or in communities throughout Thailand remains unknown.This project aims to improve access to humanitarian assistance and protection services for the most vulnerable migrants in detention in Thailand. Over a period of twelve months, IOM is providing life-saving assistance in the form of primary healthcare service provision, mental health and psychosocial support, food, non-food items and water, sanitation and hygiene support. IOM aims to ensure that at-risk groups detained in Immigration Detention Centres and other government facilities have adequate access to material and non-material support and basic services that meet their immediate needs.</narrative>
  </description>
